    plugins: replaceable engagement and safety fallback behind a host floor
    
    Second step of docs/design/xpilot-replaceable-core.md, plan from
    committee X-20261023-12 (round 2), floors decided by the user on
    2026-10-23. sp_engagement and sp_safety_fallback are the always-on
    defaults (priority 10000); with them selfdrived is SP's own.
    
    When a plugin owns role:engagement.state or role:safety.fallback,
    openpilot/tesla/core_host.py:
    - runs SP's update_events first, lets the fallback edit the events in
      place, then puts every floor event back (pedals, cancel, FCW, stock
      AEB, panda/CAN faults, core-data liveness, selfdrivedLagging, DM,
      lkasDisable);
    - clamps the plugin's state machine and MADS: floor disables win, an
      engage needs SP's driver ENABLE (MADS: LKAS button), active matches
      enabled and the state, soft disable ends within 4 s, ET.PERMANENT is
      always shown, the LKAS button disengages MADS, the Mads param rules;
    - latches both axes off for the rest of the drive on a raise or an
      invalid output, also across selfdrived restarts, and shows the new
      xpilotPluginFault alert (cereal: appended OnroadEventSP @29);
    - card sends only while selfdriveState is alive, so a hung selfdrived
      stops actuation within about 0.1 s instead of the 2-3 s of cached
      carControl.
    
    SP files: one install_core call in selfdrived.py, one core_alive call
    on card.py's existing send condition.
    
    phase4_check.py lists the failure cases C1-C12 and passes on this host
    with stub zmq/msgq/setproctitle/Params modules (pure-python paths only);
    7 mutations of core_host.py are each caught. All other *_check.py also
    pass there. Not built, not run on a device, not driven.
    
